@style(IDocDemo)
@title(Algorithms and automata)

Every block on this page is live in the preview. Each feature gets one line and one working example; the block is also what prints in the PDF.

@section(Algorithm animations: sorting and graph search)

Watch a sorting algorithm or a graph search run one step at a time, as a bar chart or a node-and-edge diagram.

@algoviz
algo: bubble
data: 5 2 8 1 9 3
@end

@b{Sorting.} algo: bubble, insertion, selection, quick or merge, with data: a list of numbers.

@algoviz
algo: merge
data: 9 4 7 1 3
@end

@b{Graph search.} algo: bfs, dfs or dijkstra, then one edge per line (A-B, or A-B: 7 for a weight) and start:.

@algoviz
algo: dijkstra
A-B: 1
B-C: 2
A-C: 5
start: A
@end

@b{A graded exercise.} expect= names what the run produces: final= (the sorted list), order= (the visit order), steps= (frames taken) or dist(NODE)=.

@algoviz(id=sort1, submit=server, expect="final=1,2,5,8")
algo: insertion
data: 5 2 8 1
@end

@b{Graded.} The server runs the same algorithm on the handed-in source and reads the same things: the final list, the visit order, a distance or the step count.

@section(Finite automata and Turing machines)

Describe a DFA, NFA or Turing machine as a list of transitions and get its state diagram, which you can run on a test string step by step.

@automaton
type: dfa
start: q0
accept: q1
q0 -0-> q1
q0 -1-> q0
q1 -0-> q1
q1 -1-> q0
@end

@b{The lines.} type: dfa, nfa or tm (default dfa).

@automaton
type: nfa
start: s
accept: f
s -a-> s
s -a-> f
s -eps-> f
@end

@b{Turing machines.} q0 a/b,R q1 reads a, writes b, moves the head R, L or S, and goes to q1.

@automaton
type: tm
start: q0
accept: qa
q0 1/0,R q0
q0 _/_,S qa
@end

@b{A preset test string.} input= fills the test box when the page opens.

@automaton(input=0110)
start: q0
accept: q0
q0 -0-> q0
q0 -1-> q0
@end

@b{A graded exercise.} accepts= and rejects= list example strings; each one is a check.

Accept strings ending in 0.
@automaton(id=end0, pts=3, accepts="0,10,110", rejects="1,01")
start: q0
@end

@b{Graded.} Each accepts= and rejects= string is one check, and the score is the fraction the student's machine gets right, so a machine that is right on four of five examples earns four fifths.


@section(Every other argument)

@b{Algoviz look and timing.} theme= sets the look, width= and height= size it, time= is the live-class window.

@algoviz(theme=showcase, width=640, height=320, time=5m)
algo: insertion
data: 7 3 9 1 4
@end

@b{Merge sort and depth-first search.} The other two algorithms the visualizer steps through.

@algoviz
algo: merge
data: 6 2 9 4 1 8
@end

@algoviz(theme=studio)
algo: dfs
A-B
A-C
B-D
start: A
@end

@b{Automaton layout and look.} output= places the diagram, noedit hides the rules, theme= sets the look, width= and height= size it, time= is the live-class window.

@automaton(output=right, theme=dark, width=640, height=300, time=5m)
type: dfa
start: q0
accept: q1
q0 -1-> q1
q0 -0-> q0
q1 -0-> q1
q1 -1-> q0
@end

@automaton(noedit, input=11)
type: dfa
start: q0
accept: q1
q0 -1-> q1
q1 -1-> q0
@end
